  "response": "The most likely cause is hidden wall-clock coupling in `src/billing.ts`: seven direct `Date.now()`/`new Date()` calls, several inside the 204-line `generateInvoice`. If execution or a test crosses midnight, those calls can observe different dates within one invoice calculation.\n\nRelated risks reinforce this diagnosis:\n\n- `src/date.ts` mixes UTC and local methods, so behavior varies by host timezone.\n- It parses timezone-less date strings, making boundary interpretation environment-dependent.\n- Billing tests provide weak coverage, so the intended boundary semantics may not be asserted.\n\nProposed fix:\n\n```ts\ntype Clock = {\n  now(): Date;\n};\n\nconst systemClock: Clock = {\n  now: () => new Date(),\n};\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  clock: Clock = systemClock,\n): Invoice {\n  const now = clock.now(); // capture exactly once\n  // Pass `now` into pure billing/date helpers.\n}\n```\n\nUse one time domain—prefer UTC for billing—and replace local getters with UTC equivalents. Require timestamps to contain `Z` or an explicit offset.\n\nThen add deterministic boundary tests using a fixed clock, covering immediately before and after midnight and, if relevant, month/year and DST transitions. Assert invoice dates and billing periods precisely rather than merely checking that an invoice exists.\n\nThe large-function finding is a secondary contributor: extracting pure date/period calculation helpers would make these cases much easier to test. The contract-drift findings are important cleanup, but they are less likely to explain temporal flakiness.",
